/*  DOCUMENTATION - CONFIG BLOCKS

	Note that the name of this block is what you need to choose with -config on
	the command line. If you don't use -config then it checks to see if there's
	only one config block in the file (excluding globals). If there is, it uses
	it, otherwise it tries for a config named "default". If that doesn't exist,
	then it fails.

	Note that descriptive text fields such as title and description are stored
	in Unicode format (these are tagged with 'w'). To use Unicode in these,
	store as UTF-8 in the value part of the field.

	Special notes on variable formats:

		Version:

			This is of the form aa.[bb].cc.dd.eeff where aa = external version,
			bb = internal version, cc = year, dd = month, ee = day, and ff =
			build of the day. This wacky format is required to work with MS RAID
			for bug tracking.

			Optionally, you can use a filename instead of all of this, and the
			code will get the version data from the file. This is useful for
			setting minimum required builds and such, where you can just tell it
			to take whatever build you've got and use that version.

		Priority:

			This is one of User, Patch, Expansion, Language and Factory, with an
			optional +/- <number> to adjust it up or down. Example: User + 10,
			User - 50, etc.

				FACTORY   = 0x0000,		// GPG-issued "factory configured" tank
				LANGUAGE  = 0x1000,		// GPG-issued language pack
				EXPANSION = 0x2000,		// GPG-issued expansion pack tank
				PATCH     = 0x3000,		// some kind of patch tank
				USER      = 0x4000,		// user-constructed tank

		Tank Flags:

			This is a |-delimited list of tank flags. Currently supported flags:

			allow_multiplayer_xfer

				this tank may be xfer'd during a multiplayer game - do not
				set for large tanks!)

	Other special notes:

		Any block can have a retail_ok or dev_ok flag in it that controls
		whether or not the block is included if the -dev option is or is not
		included on the command line. If -dev is not included, then it is a
		retail build.

	Include/Exclude Specs:

		Where they are allowed, there can be as many include* and exclude*
		entries as you like. Each is a simple DOS-style wildcard pattern match
		on the filename being considered. Multiple patterns can be put in one
		entry for convenience - just comma-delimit them.

		A pattern is broken down into a path pattern component and a file
		pattern component (each of which may use DOS-style wildcards). Each
		component is tested separately against the candidate. Here are some
		sample patterns:

			*.avi,*.mpg			<< any AVI or MPG file will match
			world\				<< match anything in the world directory
			art\*\*.bmp			<< any BMP under the art subdir
			world\*\??.skr*		<< any skr* file with a 2-char name under world

		In order for a file to be selected, it must match any of the include*
		entries, and it must not match any of the exclude* entries. If there
		are no include* entries, then all files are considered for inclusion,
		and if there are no exclude* entries, then no files that are included
		will be excluded.

	Include/Exclude Lists:

		Where include* and exclude* are allowed, you can have include_list* and
		exclude_list* entries. These are the same as include/exclude specs
		(comma-delimited lists) except rather than a pattern match they match
		files out of a list. The list may either be a filename or a fuel block
		name.
		
		If it's a file, then each line from the file is used as an additional
		include/exclude pattern to match. The parser knows about the DS format
		of file journaling as well, which looks like this:

			Open: <filename>

		If it's a fuel block, then each entry from the referenced fuel block is
		used as an additional include/exclude pattern to match. The format of
		this block must look like this:

		[block_name]
		{
			* = filename1.txt;
			* = filename2.psd, filename3.log;
		}

	Formats:

		Currently lzo and zlib are supported. The default format is raw. This
		format should be used for non-compressable file types like mp3 and wav.

		The LZO compressor is under the GPL so we cannot add it to our tool and
		then release the tool without opening the source as well. Instead, the
		tank builder looks for a special LZO dll and if it's not found, LZO is
		not supported as a format. Only GPG has the LZO dll, and we cannot
		release this without violating the GPL. If you want to write your own,
		create a dll called GPLZO.DLL and put it in the same dir as the tank
		builder. Give it a single export that fills this signature:

			int __stdcall Deflate( void*        out,
								   unsigned int outSize,
								   const void*  in,
								   unsigned int inSize );

			Export name: _Deflate@16

		It should return the number of bytes stored in the output buffer. Maybe
		one day we'll write our own version of the LZO compressor, but until
		then gotta do this workaround.

		The zlib library is 100% free and is therefore part of the tank builder.

	Filters:

		Here are all available filters and the parameters they support:

		create_raw_texture

			This creates a RAW format texture from whatever is read in. Params:

			mipmaps			- set to true to build mip-maps into the texture
			strip_mip0		- set to true to strip off mip-map 0 (useful for reducing art size easily)

		scan_dollars

			This scans for text files that contain $$$ and warns for each line
			it finds.

	SAMPLE CONFIG BLOCK

	[config_name]
	{
		// here are all possible options for a configuration

	w   file			= "MyTankFile.tank";                // name of output filename for created tank (optional, defaults to <config_name>.dsres if missing, or can be overridden by -tank on command line, and output dir can be overridden by -out on command line)
	w   title			= "Reservoir Dogs Mod for DS";      // short title to use by GUI or whatever (optional, defaults to empty)
	w   author			= "Baz & Tel";                      // author name (optional, defaults to currently logged in user name)
	w   description		= "Blah blah blah...";              // description, can be as long as you like and contain whatever you want (optional, defaults to empty)
		priority		= User + 10;                        // priority level of this tank (optional, default is user)
		product_version	= 1.[0].01.04.2802;                 // version of product this tank was built with (optional, defaults to tankbuilder version)
		minimum_version	= dungeonsiege.exe;                 // minimum version of product required to use this tank (optional, defaults to 0's)
		tank_flags		= allow_multiplayer_xfer;           // flags to set on this tank (optional, defaults to none)
		is_gpg			= false;                            // did gpg make this tank? used for setting creator field (optional, defaults to false)
		specializes		= some_other_config_name;           // which config should we base this one on? (optional, defaults to none)

		// there can be at most one input_global block in a config. this is used
		// as the base to copy all input* blocks from. this is a good place to
		// put global exclusions, and set the input_root to base everything on.
		// note that no actual files are pulled in using an input_global block,
		// this just sets up a base. use an input* block to pull in files.

		[input_global]
		{
			input_root		= p:\tattoo_assets\cdimage\world+;  // root path to select files from for this input block - add a '+' for recursive search (optional, defaults to current dir, or can be overridden by -in on command line)
			output_root		= world\;                           // root path to store selected files in within the tank (optional, defaults to root dir of tank)
			include*		= world\*\regions\*\*.gas;          // pattern match of files to include when iterating through input_root (optional, defaults to "all files")
			exclude*		= *.bak;                            // pattern match of files to exclude when iterating through input_root (optional, defaults to "exclude none")
			exclude_zero*	= *.gas;							// pattern match of files to exclude if they are zero-length
			exclude_list*	= exclude_block;					// one or more include lists to base off this input root
			include_list*	= files.log;						// one or more include lists to base off this input root
		}

		// input blocks are meant to select files to include in the tank. there
		// can be as many of these as necessary - they are considered in order.

		[input*]
		{
			// this block has the same formatting as input_global, with one
			// exception - the input_root can start with %base_root%, which will
			// substitute the base's input_root there. this is useful for
			// setting the global at the root of the cdimage and building all
			// the configs off of that. note that it ignores the base root's
			// recursion flag of course.

			input_root	= %base_root%\maps\map_world;       // actually p:\tattoo_assets\cdimage\world\maps\map_world, non-recursive (optional, defaults to current dir)
		}

		// format blocks determine how the data of files are stored in a tank.
		// usually this tunes compression. there may be any number of
		// format_global* blocks - they are all separate and additive. files are
		// run through all format blocks, and they are considered in the order
		// that they appear in the file, with format_global* blocks first, then
		// the format* blocks next. the last one to match "wins" and sets the
		// format.

		[format_global*]
		{
			// this block has the same formatting as input_global for include*,
			// exclude*, and exclude_zero* entries. here is the new stuff:

			format					= lzo;					// name of the format, see above (required)
			compression_level		= 7;					// compression level 0-10 - 0 is max speed, 10 is max compression, -1 for algorithm choose default level (optional, defaults to -1, currently ignored by lzo)
			chunk_size				= 16384;				// size of chunks to compress data in - 0 for none, where streaming is ok, -1 for default - must be a multiple of system page size, currently 4096 bytes (optional, defaults to -1)
			min_compression_ratio	= 10;					// minimum compression ratio in percent required to accept the file using a compressed format - 0 for don't use, -1 for default (optional, defaults to -1, which is 5% i think)
			include*				= ...;
			exclude*				= ...;
			exclude_zero*			= ...;
		}

		// format* blocks are meant to set formatting just like format_global*
		// blocks, see rules for format_global* for more info.

		[format*]
		{
			format					= zlib;
			// ...
		}

		// filter blocks specify filters to run on data as it goes into a tank.
		// filters may change the data or analyze it. the available filters are
		// set up in the tank builder app. same rules apply here as for the
		// format blocks except it's cumulative. everybody wins, and filters are
		// applied in successive order - first the globals in the order they
		// appear in the gas file, and then the filter blocks for the chosen
		// configuration.

		[filter_global*]
		{
			// this block has the same formatting as input_global for include*,
			// exclude*, and exclude_zero* entries. here is the new stuff:
			filter					= create_raw_texture;	// name of the filter, see above (required)
			mipmaps					= true;					// params for the filter
			include*				= ...;
			exclude*				= ...;
			exclude_zero*			= ...;
		}

		[filter*]
		{
			filter					= create_raw_texture;
			// ...
		}
	}

	// $$$$ add support for direct file lists, either as inline or external.
	//      and ignore duplicates. and support journal-based ordering as well.

	// $$$$ add support for version being read from gas block (use fuel address)
	//      so we can use the content build numbers if need it.

	// $$$$ add support for building alias lists. either put them in here
	//      directly or read them from a gas block (use fuel address) so we can
	//      configure it from the same place the game gets it.

	// $$$$ file lists need to support aliased types!

	BENCHMARK NOTES FOR LZO:

	-- taken with 0601 build --

	test platform: p2-450 w/ 256 megs, voodoo3 16 megs. only difference between
	tanks was lzo vs. zlib used for all compressable files. rebooted between lzo
	and zlib tests. ran three tests in series so the first test is uncached and
	the second and third are cached. test used "map=map_world noloadintro=true"
	on retail fullscreen.

	ZLIB: 33 secs, 27 secs, 27 secs
	LZO:  27 secs, 22 secs, 23 secs

	the same relative percentage was found informally on 2GHz P4.

	conclusion: LZO is about 20% faster than zlib. it's fast enough that its
	uncached performance is the same as zlib's cached performance.
*/

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

[aliases]
{
	tank = dsres, dsmap;
}

[globals]
{
	author				= "Gas Powered Games";

	[input_global]
	{
		exclude*		= vssver.scc, pspbrwse.jbf, *.bak, *.tmp, *.??~, *.%tank%, *.dll, *.exe, *.rtf, *.log, *.pdb, *.obj, *.ini;
		exclude*		= *\x-inventory\*\;
		exclude*		= *\z_old\*\;
		exclude*		= config\fortune\*.gas, config\fortune\*.lqd*;
		exclude_zero*	= *.gas;

		input_root		= p:\work\ds1\main\cdimage\+;
	}

	[format_global*]
	{
		// $$$ NOTE: when we start using lzo again, switch .gas to use zlib only!!
//		format			= lzo;		// $$ TEMPORARILY DISABLED
		format			= zlib;
		include*		= *;
		exclude*		= *.wav, *.mp3, *.bik, *.avi;
	}

	[filter_global*]
	{
		filter			= create_raw_texture;
		mipmaps			= true;
		include*		= art\bitmaps\*\*.%img%;
		exclude*		= art\bitmaps\gui\*\;
		exclude*		= art\bitmaps\sfx\*\;
	}

	[filter_global*]
	{
		filter			= create_raw_texture;
		mipmaps			= false;
		include*		= art\bitmaps\gui\*\*.%img%;
		include*		= art\bitmaps\sfx\*\*.%img%;
	}

	[filter_global*]
	{
		dev_ok			= false;
		filter			= scan_dollars;
		include*		= *.gas, *.skrit;
	}

	is_gpg				= true;
	product_version		= dungeonsiege.exe;
	minimum_version		= dungeonsiege.exe;
}

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

[terrain]
{
	file				= "Terrain.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Terrain Art Assets";
	specializes			= globals;

	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\art\terrain+;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\art\bitmaps\terrain+;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\world\global\siege_nodes+;
	}
}

[objects]
{
	file				= "Objects.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Game Object Art Assets";
	specializes			= globals;

	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\art\animations+;
		exclude*	= art\animations\skrits\*\;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\art\bitmaps+;
		exclude*	= art\bitmaps\terrain\*\;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\art\meshes+;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\movies+;
	}
}

[sound]
{
	file				= "Sound.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Sound Assets";
	specializes			= globals;

	[input*]
	{
		input_root	= %base_root%\sound+;
		exclude*	= sound\voices\*\;
	}
}

[voices]
{
	file				= "Voices.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Localizable Voice Assets";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\sound\voices+;
}

[logic]
{
	file				= "Logic.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Logic and Configuration Files";
	specializes			= globals;

	[input*]
	{
		include*		= \*;
		include*		= art\*;

		include*		= art\animations\skrits\*\;
		include*		= auto\*\;
		include*		= config\*\;
		exclude*		= config\editor\*.gas, config\editor\*.lqd*;
		include*		= ui\*\;
		include*		= world\ai\*\;
		include*		= world\contentdb\*\;
		include*		= world\global\*\;
		exclude*		= world\global\siege_nodes\*\;
		exclude*		= config\dev\*\;
		exclude*		= \*\system_detail.gas;			// this must be done explicitly because the path is remapped
	}

	[input*]
	{
		output_root		= config\;
		include*		= system_detail.gas;
	}

	[input*]
	{
		// $ just include the maps dir all by itself so the dir always exists
		//   even when no map tanks are mounted.

		input_root		= %base_root%\world\maps;
	}
}

[dev_logic]
{
	file				= "DevLogic.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Core: Special Dev-Only Logic and Configuration Files";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\config\dev+;
}

[se_extras]
{
	file				= "SiegeEditorExtras.dsres";
	title				= "Siege Editor: Extra Configuration Files";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\config\editor;
}

[map_world]
{
	file				= "World.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: The World of Ehb";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\map_world+;
}

[multiplayer_world]
{
	file				= "MpWorld.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Multiplayer World";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\multiplayer_world+;
}

[marsh_test]
{
	file				= "MarshTest.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Marsh's Test Map";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\marsh_test+;
}

[multiplayer_scott_test]
{
	file				= "MpScottTest.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Scott's Multiplayer Test Map";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\multiplayer_scott_test+;
}

[monster_museum]
{
	file				= "MonsterMuseum.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Monster Museum";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\monster_museum+;
}

[object_museum]
{
	file				= "ObjectMuseum.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Object Museum";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\object_museum+;
}

[gp_stress]
{
	file				= "StressTest.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Multiplayer Stress Test";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\gp_stress+;
}

[uber_test]
{
	file				= "UberTest.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Uber Test Map";
	specializes			= globals;

	input*:input_root	= %base_root%\world\maps\uber_test+;
}

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

[yesterhaven_map]
{
	file				= "Yesterhaven.dsmap";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Map: Yesterhaven";
	specializes			= globals;
	minimum_version		= 00.[05].2002.03.0801;				// rtm build

	[input*]
	{
		input_root		= %base_root%\world\maps\yesterhaven+;
	}
	[input*]
	{
		input_root		= %base_root%\;
		include*		= $required;
	}
}

[yesterhaven_mod]
{
	file				= "Yesterhaven.dsres";
	title				= "Dungeon Siege Mod: files required for Yesterhaven";
	specializes			= globals;
	minimum_version		= 00.[05].2002.03.0801;				// rtm build
	priority			= EXPANSION;

	[input*]
	{
		input_root		= %base_root%\+;
		exclude*		= world\maps\yesterhaven\*\;
		exclude*		= lqd_dictionary.*;
	}
}

